 ],
 "literal": "A pond heron roosts on a straw stack / in the beautiful fields of blue water lilies where paddy-reaping laborers / drink the toddy from wide-leaved water lilies that have blossomed from buds, and to the clear sea's / roaring waves' sweet rhythm and beat, they sway / in the soft-land settlements of your good country, O warrior! / Desiring many fruits, ascending into the shrinking sky, / making the clefts of the great mountains resound, having approached / a great tree possessing fruit, because it is finished, having become helpless / like a flock of birds that returns without getting, your / desire bringing me, having come to praise your fame, I, the seeker of gifts, / shall I return empty-handed, O one who excels with the sword? / Even if you do not give, I will not grieve. / May you be free of suffering, O great lord! Between us, / may we see each other closely and frequently every day, / in the presence of your honey-sweet-spoken wife with fragrant, thick-growing hair / and choice jewels, on your chest, / O Murukaṉ-like warrior who desires battle, in your joyful court!",
 "translation": "Like a flock of birds that flies high into the shrinking sky, desiring abundant fruit, making the mountain clefts resound as they head towards a great fruit-bearing tree, only to return empty-handed and helpless when they find the fruit is gone—so have I come, drawn by desire, to sing your praise, O seeker of gifts. Shall I return empty-handed, O hero who excels with the sword? Even if you give me nothing, I shall not grieve. May you live free of illness, O lord! May we meet closely and daily in your joyful court, O Murukaṉ-like warrior who loves battle, whose chest is embraced by your honey-tongued wife with fragrant, thick hair and choice jewels, in your good country of soft, fertile lands, where paddy-reaping laborers drink toddy from wide-leaved water lilies that have blossomed from buds, and sway to the sweet rhythm of the roaring waves of the clear sea, while pond herons roost on the straw stacks.",
 "modern": "வயல்களில் நெல் அறுக்கும் தொழிலாளர்கள், மொட்டவிழ்ந்த ஆம்பல் மலர்களின் அகன்ற இலைகளில் உள்ள கள்ளைக் குடித்துவிட்டு, தெளிந்த கடலின் அலைகளின் ஓசைக்கு ஏற்ப ஆடும் மென்புல நாட்டை உடையவனே! பழங்களை விரும்பி, வானில் பறந்து, மலைப் பிளவுகள் அதிருமாறு சென்று, பழங்கள் தீர்ந்துபோன மரத்தைக் கண்டு ஏமாற்றத்துடன் திரும்பும் பறவைக் கூட்டம் போல, உன்னிடம் பரிசு பெற விரும்பி வந்த நான் வறுமையுடன் திரும்புவேனோ? நீ பரிசில் தராவிட்டாலும் நான் வருந்த மாட்டேன். என் தலைவனே! நீ நோய் நொடியின்றி வாழ்வாயாக! நறுமணமும் அடர்த்தியும் கொண்ட கூந்தலையும், தேன் போன்ற மொழியையும், சிறந்த அணிகலன்களையும் உடைய உன் மனைவியைத் தழுவிய மார்பை உடையவனே! போரை விரும்பும் சேயோன் போன்றவனே! உன்னுடைய மகிழ்ச்சியான கொலுவீற்றிருக்கும் இடத்தில் நாம் நாள்தோறும் நெருக்கமாகக் காண்போமாக!",
 "explanation": "The poet Peruṉtalaiic Cāttaṉār addresses the chieftain Mūvaṉ in this poem of the Puṟam genre (tiṇai: pāṭāṇ, tuṟai: paricil kaṭānilai). The poet compares himself to a flock of birds that flies eagerly toward a fruit-bearing tree, only to find it bare and return disappointed. He asks the chieftain if he must return empty-handed after coming with great hope to sing his praises. Even if the chieftain does not grant him gifts, the poet generously wishes him a long life free of illness. He describes the rich agricultural landscape of Mūvaṉ's country, where laborers drink toddy and dance to the rhythm of the sea waves, contrasting this fertility with his own empty-handedness. Finally, the poet expresses a desire to remain in the chieftain's joyful court, enjoying his company daily alongside his beautiful, sweet-spoken wife.",
